Tuning a Circuit Breaker during high load, traffic spikes, or huge traffic scenarios is critical to prevent cascading failures and maintain system resilience. Here’s how you approach it:

✅ 1. Understand Circuit Breaker Basics

Closed: All requests pass through.
Open: Requests are blocked after failure threshold is reached.
Half-Open: Test requests allowed to check recovery.


✅ 2. Key Parameters to Tune

Failure Rate Threshold

Default: 50%
For high load: Increase slightly (e.g., 60–70%) to avoid premature opening during transient spikes.


Sliding Window Size

COUNT-based or TIME-based.
For huge traffic: Use time-based (e.g., 10s) instead of count-based to smooth out spikes.


Minimum Number of Calls

Increase this during high traffic (e.g., 100 → 500) so the breaker doesn’t trip on a small sample.


Wait Duration in Open State

Reduce slightly (e.g., from 60s → 30s) to allow quicker recovery checks.


Permitted Calls in Half-Open

Increase cautiously (e.g., 10 → 20) to validate recovery faster under load.




✅ 3. Add Bulkhead + Rate Limiter

During spikes, combine Bulkhead (limit concurrent threads) and RateLimiter (limit requests/sec) with Circuit Breaker.
Example decorator chain:
Timeout → Bulkhead → RateLimiter → CircuitBreaker → Retry




✅ 4. Use Time-Based Sliding Window

COUNT-based can trip too fast during bursts.
TIME-based (e.g., 10s window) smooths out short-lived spikes.


✅ 5. Monitor Metrics

Watch:

Failure rate


Rejected calls
State transitions


Use Micrometer + Prometheus/Grafana for alerts.


✅ 6. Dynamic Configuration

Use Spring Cloud Config + @RefreshScope or Centralized Config to adjust thresholds without redeploying.


